      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The narrowest bony dimension of the adult female pelvis (obstetric conjugate) is ≈110 mm, and even the widest transverse pelvic outlet achievable with full muscular relaxation is ≈120–130 mm.",
      "evidence": "Obstetric textbooks and radiographic studies of pelvic dimensions (Cunningham et al., 2014; studies of pelvic outlet expansion during second‑stage labor).",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The skull’s smallest transverse diameter (140 mm) is larger than the maximal transverse pelvic opening (≈130 mm) by at least 10 mm.",
      "evidence": "Direct numerical comparison of steps 1 and 2.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Fully fused adult cranial bone is rigid; cortical bone tolerates only ~2 % compressive strain before fracture, so a 7 % reduction (140 mm → ≤130 mm) would cause skull fracture.",
      "evidence": "Biomechanical properties of cortical bone reported in orthopedic literature (e.g., Currey, 2002; fracture thresholds for human skull bone).",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the skull is larger than the pelvic aperture and cannot be deformed enough without breaking, an intact adult skull cannot pass through the birth canal.",
      "evidence": "Logical synthesis of steps 3 and 4.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
